# Deployed as .htaccess beside the web client's index.html. The bundle files
# carry content hashes and can cache forever; index.html must always
# revalidate, or a phone shows last week's app against this week's data.
<Files "index.html">
  Header set Cache-Control "no-cache"
</Files>
<FilesMatch "\.(js|png)$">
  Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
</FilesMatch>
<Files "favicon.ico">
  Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600"
</Files>
# The manifest: Apache has no type for .webmanifest out of the box, and a
# manifest served as text/plain is one a browser can refuse to install from.
# It revalidates like index.html — the icons and name are allowed to change.
<Files "manifest.webmanifest">
  AddType application/manifest+json .webmanifest
  Header set Cache-Control "no-cache"
</Files>

# The service worker itself must NEVER be cached immutably, or the *.js rule
# above would make a broken one permanent: the browser would keep serving the
# worker it already has and there would be no way to ship a replacement. This
# comes after that rule deliberately, so it wins. It is also what makes
# tools/sw-kill.js a usable escape hatch rather than a theory.
<Files "sw.js">
  Header set Cache-Control "no-cache"
</Files>

# NO HOST ROUTING HERE — and it was here, and it took the sandbox down.
#
# This file began as a copy of CalMind's web.htaccess, which carries CalMind's
# subdomain routing: on a `test.` host, rewrite everything under this
# directory to /test/calmind/. That block is CalMind's — it exists so
# /home/public/calmind's own per-directory config re-applies the site's
# test→/test/ mapping for CalMind's OWN path. In ChefMind's directory it was
# dead code on the apex (the host never matches) and a live wrong answer on
# test.seancheren.com: every request under /ChefMind was served CalMind's
# index.html and CalMind's bundle, while TestChefMind's files sat correctly on
# disk beside it. Found by TestChefMind's first qdtp, 2026-09-15 — the
# served-page proof said "that is not TestChefMind's page", and it was right.
#
# The site's root .htaccess already maps test.seancheren.com/ChefMind to
# /test/ChefMind before this file is consulted, so this directory needs no
# routing of its own; only the cache rules above. (ChefMind's production copy
# still carries the CalMind block — harmless there, latent everywhere else.)
